"A cyclic non-Ramsey heptagon (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.23327v1)\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.23327v1\",\"lineno\":563}); Total lines: 693\nL542: For $0<t<1/10$, its signs at $-\\infty,-1,-1/3,0,1$ are respectively $-,+,-,+,-$. Hence it has four distinct real roots, giving four distinct unit roots of $f_{-}$. Negative $t$ follows by conjugation. If a unit root were shared by $f_{+}$ and $f_{-}$, subtraction would give\nL543: \nL544:  | $$z=\\frac{2u^{2}}{3u^{2}-1},$$  |\nL545: \nL546: whose modulus is $1$ only if $u^{2}=1$. Hence the seven points are distinct throughout the stated interval.\nL547: Choose a real derivation with $D(t)=1$ and extend it to $\\mathbb{C}$ by $D(x+iy)=D(x)+iD(y)$. The equal coefficients of $z^{3}$ and $z^{2}$ in $f_{+}$ and $f_{-}$ give equality of the first two power sums of their root multisets. Consequently the weights annihilate the constant, linear, and quadratic coordinate functions. The weighted product of their roots is $-1$, because their constant coefficients are $u^{2}$ and $-u^{2}$. For a unit root $z_{j}$, write $D(z_{j})=ib_{j}z_{j}$ with $b_{j}$ real.\nL548: Differentiating the product ratio gives\nL549:  | $$\\sum_{j}\\lambda_{j}b_{j}=0.$$  |\nL550: \nL551: Differentiating the weighted first coordinate moments gives $\\sum_{j}\\lambda_{j}D(p_{j})=0$. Since the weights are integers, differentiating the quadratic moments shows that $M=\\sum_{j}\\lambda_{j}D(p_{j})p_{j}^{\\mathsf{T}}$ is skew-symmetric. The preceding angular identity makes its skew-symmetric part zero, so $M=0$. Thus all moment conditions of the invariant criterion hold.\nL552: It remains to check that the energy is nonzero. Let $z_{j}(t)$ denote the algebraic root branches and put $E(t)=\\sum_{j}\\lambda_{j}|z_{j}^{\\prime}(t)|^{2}$. At a transcendental parameter $t$, implicit differentiation gives $D(z_{j}(t))=z_{j}^{\\prime}(t)$, so $E(t)$ is the required invariant energy. The root $1$ of $f_{+}$ is constant.\nL553: At $t=0$, the ordinary angular velocities of its other two roots are $2,2$, while the angular velocities of the four roots of $f_{-}$, ordered as $1,-1,e^{i\\pi/3},e^{-i\\pi/3}$, are $-2,2,2,2$. These values follow directly by implicit differentiation; for example $u^{\\prime}(0)=2i$. Therefore\nL554:  | $$\\lim_{t\\to 0}E(t)=2\\cdot 2^{2}-4\\cdot 2^{2}=-8.$$  |\nL555: In fact $E(t)$ is a rational function over $\\mathbb{Q}(i)$: for each simple unit root, $|z_{j}^{\\prime}|^{2}=-(z_{j}^{\\prime}/z_{j})^{2}$, and implicit differentiation expresses $z_{j}^{\\prime}$ rationally in $t,z_{j}$. The sum over the roots of each polynomial is symmetric; for $f_{+}$ use only its quadratic factor, since the doubled root $1$ contributes zero. The limit $-8$ shows that this rational function is not identically zero.\nL556: It therefore cannot vanish at a transcendental $t$, and simplicity of the roots excludes poles on the stated interval. Lemma cite45†A.1 proves non-Ramseyness, and the preceding colour bound with $L=4$ gives eight colours. ∎\nL557: ### A common colouring for many parameters\nL558: \nL559: Let $S\\subset(2,\\infty)$ be algebraically independent over $\\mathbb{Q}$. For every dimension there is a single twelve-colouring avoiding all the configurations $P_{r}$ from Theorem cite59†1 , simultaneously for $r\\in S$. Extend $S$ to a transcendence basis and prescribe\nL560: \nL561:  | $$D(r)=\\sqrt{(2r-1)(2r-3)(2r-4)}\\quad(r\\in S).$$  |\nL562: The calculation in the main proof now gives the same invariant $-24$ for every $P_{r}$ with the unscaled function $F(q)=\\|D(q)\\|^{2}$. Consequently $\\lfloor F(q)/4\\rfloor\\bmod 12$ avoids them all. The set $S$ may be chosen dense in $(2,\\infty)$ and of cardinality $|\\mathbb{R}|$: choose a countable algebraically independent dense subset, extend it to a transcendence basis, and move each additional basis element into $(2,\\infty)$ by adding a suitable rational number.\nL563: ## Appendix H Arbitrary quadratic jets in one derivation\nL564: \nL565: Higher derivatives do not evade the six-point obstruction, even when their quadratic combination is indefinite.\nL566: ###### Theorem A.15.\nL567: \nL568: Let $P$ be a spherical set of at most six points, let $D$ be a derivation, and let $(c_{ab})_{0\\leq a,b\\leq k}$ be a real symmetric matrix. Put\nL569: \nL570:  | $$F_{n}(q)=\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\sum_{a,b=0}^{k}c_{ab}D^{a}(q_{j})D^{b}(q_{j}),\\qquad D^{0}(q_{j})=q_{j}.$$  |\nL571: \nL572: If fixed real weights $\\lambda_{p}$ satisfy $\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}F_{n}(p^{\\prime})=K$ for every congruent copy of $P$ in every ambient dimension, then $K=0$.\nL573: ###### Proof.\nL574: The zero quadratic form is immediate. After deleting zero rows and columns, let $k$ be the largest index occurring in the coefficient matrix. If $k=0$ (which also covers $D=0$ after discarding the positive-order terms), then translation invariance of $c_{00}\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}\\|p+t\\|^{2}$ forces $\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}=0$ and $\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}p=0$. On a centred spherical copy its value is therefore zero. Hence assume $D\\neq 0$ and $k\\geq 1$.\nL575: Choose a centred copy whose affine span is a coordinate subspace; all its derivative vectors remain in that subspace. We shall use arbitrary finite formal jets of translations and rotations. This is legitimate: choosing $u$ with $D(u)\\neq 0$, the matrix $(D^{a}(u^{b}))_{0\\leq a,b\\leq m}$ has determinant $\\bigl(\\prod_{b=0}^{m}b!\\bigr)D(u)^{m(m+1)/2}$. Rational combinations of its columns are dense in $\\mathbb{R}^{m+1}$.\nL576: Thus actual scalar jets are dense among all jets; applying this entrywise to skew matrices and using the Cayley chart gives the same statement for orthogonal jets. Polynomial identities therefore extend to the formal jets used below.\nL577: Write $Z_{j}=\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}D^{j}p$. The quadratic translation terms give $\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}=0$. The linear terms give $\\sum_{b}c_{ab}Z_{b}=0$ for every $a$, also after every formal rotation. Choose $a$ with $c_{ak}\\neq 0$ and insert $A(t)=\\exp(st^{r}T/r!)$, where $T$ is skew. The coefficient of $s$ is\nL578: \nL579:  | $$\\sum_{b\\geq r}\\binom{b}{r}c_{ab}TZ_{b-r}=0.$$  |\nL580: \nL581: Taking $r=k,k-1,\\ldots,1$ successively, and then using the original row equation, proves\nL582: \nL583:  | $$Z_{0}=Z_{1}=\\cdots=Z_{k}=0.$$  |  | (A.11)\nL584: We next extract the two needed rotational conditions. Use the symbol $c(U,V)=\\sum c_{ab}U^{a}V^{b}$. The symbol $(U+V)^{m}$ represents $D^{m}\\|q\\|^{2}$, so contributes nothing to a rotational variation. Subtract such symbols, degree by degree. If nothing remains, the value at every point of the centred sphere is the same, and $K=0$. Otherwise, let $h$ be the highest nonzero homogeneous part remaining, of degree $N$. Subtracting a further multiple of $(U+V)^{N}$, we may assume\nL585:  | $$h(U,0)=h(0,V)=0,\\qquad h\\neq 0.$$  |\nL586: \nL587: In particular $N\\geq 2$.\nL588: \nL589: Let $E$ be the coordinate span of the centred configuration, let $e$ be a unit normal, and, for $v,w\\in E$, define skew maps by\nL590: \nL591:  | $$Sx=e(v\\cdot x)-(e\\cdot x)v,\\qquad Tx=e(w\\cdot x)-(e\\cdot x)w.$$  |\nL592: Thus, writing $f_{p}=v\\cdot p$ and $g_{p}=w\\cdot p$, we have $Sp=ef_{p}$, $Tp=eg_{p}$, and $(ST+TS)p=-vg_{p}-wf_{p}$. Choose formal scalar jets $\\theta^{(j)}(0)=x^{j}$ and $\\eta^{(j)}(0)=y^{j}$. To mixed order in $\\varepsilon,\\delta$, the orthogonal jet is\nL593: \nL594:  | $$A=I+\\varepsilon\\theta S+\\delta\\eta T+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\varepsilon\\delta\\theta\\eta(ST+TS)+O(\\varepsilon^{2},\\delta^{2}).$$  |\nL595: This is a finite formal expansion, realizable through the Cayley chart. Put $B(r,s)=\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}r_{p}s_{p}$. The product of the two first variations contributes $2c(U+x,V+y)B(f,g)$, while the two pairings with the mixed variation contribute $-c(U+x+y,V)B(f,g)-c(U,V+x+y)B(f,g)$. Hence the $\\varepsilon\\delta$ coefficient is the symbol\nL596: \nL597:  | $$2c(U+x,V+y)-c(U+x+y,V)-c(U,V+x+y)$$  |\nL598: \nL599: applied to $B(f^{(a)},g^{(b)})$.\nL600: The degree-$N$ part in $x,y$ is $2h(x,y)B(f,g)$, because $h(U,0)=h(0,V)=0$; hence $\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}pp^{\\mathsf{T}}=0$. For degree $N-1$, let $a=[U^{N-1}V]h=[UV^{N-1}]h$. Taylor expansion gives, up to multiples of the already-zero $B(f,g)$,\nL601: \nL602:  | $$\\bigl(2h_{U}-a(x+y)^{N-1}\\bigr)B(f^{\\prime},g)+\\bigl(2h_{V}-a(x+y)^{N-1}\\bigr)B(f,g^{\\prime})=0.$$  |\nL603: \nL604: Lower homogeneous components contribute at this degree only multiples of $B(f,g)$. Interchanging $x,y$ and subtracting, using the symmetry of $h$, gives\nL605:  | $$2(h_{U}-h_{V})(x,y)\\bigl(B(f^{\\prime},g)-B(f,g^{\\prime})\\bigr)=0.$$  |\nL606: \nL607: Now $h_{U}-h_{V}\\neq 0$, since otherwise $h$ would be a multiple of $(U+V)^{N}$, already removed. As the last parenthesis is $v^{\\mathsf{T}}(M-M^{\\mathsf{T}})w$ for $M=\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}D(p)p^{\\mathsf{T}}$, this proves that $M$ is symmetric. Consequently\nL608: \nL609:  | $$\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}pp^{\\mathsf{T}}=0,\\qquad\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}D(p)p^{\\mathsf{T}}\\text{ is symmetric}.$$  |  | (A.12)\nL610: Delete zero weights, and let $m$ and $d$ be the size and affine dimension of the remaining support. The space spanned by the constant vector and the $d$ coordinate vectors is totally isotropic for the nondegenerate form $B(a,b)=\\sum_{p}\\lambda_{p}a_{p}b_{p}$. Hence $d+1\\leq m/2\\leq 3$. An affine line meets a sphere in at most two points, so the only nontrivial possibility is a circle.\nL611: For any set of at most five distinct circle points, the evaluation functionals on quadratic polynomials are independent: separate any one point with a product of two chord equations. We are therefore left with six circle points, all weights nonzero.\nL612: Place a centred copy of the circle in the first two coordinate directions and write $p_{i}=\\rho w_{i}$, $w_{i}=(x_{i},y_{i})$, $\\|w_{i}\\|=1$, and $Dw_{i}=b_{i}Jw_{i}$. The space $W=\\operatorname{span}(\\mathbf{1},x,y)$ is now three-dimensional and totally isotropic in $\\mathbb{R}^{6}$, so $W=W^{\\perp}$. The first vector moment in (cite60†A.11 ) and the symmetry in (cite61†A.12 ) say that $b\\in W^{\\perp}$. Thus\nL613: \nL614:  | $$b_{i}=\\alpha+\\beta x_{i}+\\gamma y_{i}.$$  |\nL615: For $k=1$, this already gives $B(b,b)=0$, and the weighted sums of $\\|p\\|^{2}$, $\\langle p,Dp\\rangle$, and $\\|Dp\\|^{2}$ all vanish.\nL616: \nL617: Suppose $k\\geq 2$. The second vector moment also gives $\\sum_{i}\\lambda_{i}D^{2}w_{i}=0$. Set $z_{i}=x_{i}+iy_{i}$ and $a=(\\beta-i\\gamma)/2$, extending $D$ by $D(x+iy)=Dx+iDy$. Then\nL618: \nL619:  | $$Dz_{i}=ib_{i}z_{i},\\qquad b_{i}=\\alpha+az_{i}+\\overline{a}z_{i}^{-1}.$$  |\nL620: In $D^{2}z_{i}=(iDb_{i}-b_{i}^{2})z_{i}$, the only term of degree higher than two in $z_{i}$ is $-2a^{2}z_{i}^{3}$. All remaining terms vanish in the weighted sum by the quadratic moments. Consequently\nL621: \nL622:  | $$0=-2a^{2}\\sum_{i}\\lambda_{i}z_{i}^{3}.$$  |\nL623: The last sum is nonzero: otherwise its conjugate also vanishes, and the weights annihilate all Laurent monomials of degrees $-3$ through $3$. Their evaluation matrix at six distinct nonzero points has rank six by the Vandermonde determinant. Hence $a=0$, so all $b_{i}$ are equal. Repeated differentiation now gives $D^{j}p_{i}=A_{j}w_{i}+B_{j}Jw_{i}$, with coefficients independent of $i$. Every jet inner product is therefore constant across the six points, and its weighted sum is zero. Thus $K=0$. ∎\nL624: This concerns fixed weighted quadratic identities in one derivation, not arbitrary finite colourings. In particular, it does not establish the Ramsey property of any cyclic quadrilateral.\nL625: ## Appendix I A nonlinear finite-jet barrier\nL626: \nL627: The preceding quadratic barrier does not become weaker if one merely takes higher powers of a highest-order jet. The following observation covers a fairly broad class of polynomial attempts.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSplitting Kronecker squares, 2-decomposition numbers, Catalan Combinatorics, and the Saxl conjecture (https://arxiv.org/html/2202.03066v3)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2202.03066v3\",\"lineno\":1330}); Total lines: 1513\nL1310: Indeed, from the results on the constituents to $(1^{n})$ and $(2,1^{n-2})$, we know when they occur as constituents in $A^{2}([\\lambda])$; there are no further exceptional cases. In summary, $ag(\\lambda,\\mu)=0$ for $\\lambda,\\mu$ of width at most 2, except for $(\\lambda,\\mu)$ being one of $((2,1),(1^{3}))$, $((2,1^{2}),(2,1^{2}))$, $((2^{2}),(1^{4}))$, $((2^{2},1),(2,1^{3}))$.\nL1311: Note that the second case is the only one of a width 2 partition $\\lambda$ with $ag(\\lambda,\\lambda)>0$; this is [cite132†56 , Theorem 1.1(b)].\nL1312: ###### Remark 7.17.\nL1313: \nL1314: It would be very interesting if one could obtain a complete description of the $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ and $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ for $\\lambda\\in P_{2}(n)$.\nL1315: \nL1316: ## 8. Irreducible and homogeneous symmetric and antisymmetric products\nL1317: \nL1318: The proof of the following theorem utilises many of our earlier results, including the analysis of small depth and hook cases (which in turn depended on our introduction of 2-modular techniques).\nL1319: ###### Theorem 8.1.\nL1320: \nL1321: Any symmetric product $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ for $\\lambda\\in P(n)$ is (reducible and) inhomogeneous unless $\\lambda$ is a linear partition. Any anti-symmetric product $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ for $\\lambda\\in P(n)$ is (reducible and) inhomogeneous unless $\\lambda=(n),(n-1,1)$, $(2^{2})$, or $(3^{2})$ (up to conjugation).\nL1322: \nL1323: We have that $A^{2}([n-1,1])=[n-2,1^{2}]$ is irreducible for all $n\\geq 3$; furthermore $A^{2}([2^{2}])=[1^{4}]$ and $A^{2}([3^{2}])=[3,1^{3}]$.\nL1324: ###### Proof.\nL1325: That the listed products have the stated form can be easily checked by hand for the $n=4$ and $6$ cases and $A^{2}([n-1,1])=[n-2,1^{2}]$ follows from cite131†Proposition 5.4 . We now turn to the main part of the theorem, verifying that these are the only homogeneous products. We begin with the symmetric case. Assume that $\\lambda$ is a non-linear partition. For all $\\lambda\\in P(n)$, the trivial partition always labels a constituent of $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ with multiplicity equal to 1.\nL1326: Thus we need only note that the degree of the character of the symmetric square is $\\tfrac{1}{2}(\\chi^{2}(e)+\\chi(e))>1$ and so there must be some other, non-isomorphic, constituent as required.\nL1327: We now turn to the harder case of the anti-symmetric Kronecker products. If $\\lambda\\in P(n)$ and $n\\leq 9$ we can check the result by hand, and so we now assume that $n>9$ and $\\lambda$ is not of the form $\\lambda=(n-1,1)$, $(2^{2})$, $(3^{2})$, or a linear partition. If $\\lambda$ is a hook partition then $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ contains all constituents of the form $(n-m,1^{m})$ for $m\\equiv 2,3$ modulo $4$ (by Theorem cite109†4.1 ) and so the result follows.\nL1328: If $\\lambda\\in P_{2}(n)$ and $\\lambda\\neq(n/2,n/2)$, then the result follows from Proposition cite133†7.14 (iii) and if $\\lambda=(n/2,n/2)$ then the result follows from Theorem cite58†7.8 .\nL1329: We now consider the case that $\\lambda=(a^{b})$ is a rectangle for some $a,b>2$. We already know that $\\langle A^{2}[(a^{b})]\\mid[ab-3,1^{3}]\\rangle=1$ and so it suffices to show that $A^{2}[(a^{b})]\\neq[ab-3,1^{3}]$. To do this, we need only find a conjugacy class on which the characters do not coincide. We set $\\alpha=(a+b,1^{ab-a-b})$ if $a+b$ is odd and $\\alpha=(a+b+1,1^{ab-a-b-1})$ if $a+b$ is even. Let $g\\in S_{ab}$ be of cycle type $\\alpha$.\nL1330: Since all parts of $\\alpha$ are odd, we have that $\\chi(g)=\\chi(g^{2})$ for any character $\\chi$ of $S_{n}$. All hooks of $(a^{b})$ are strictly smaller than $a+b$ and so, by the Murnaghan–Nakayama rule, $[(a^{b})](g)=0=[(a^{b})](g^{2})$ and this implies that $A^{2}[(a^{b})](g)=0$. On the other hand, $3<a+b,a+b+1<ab-3$ and so $[ab-3,1^{3}](g)=[ab-3-|\\alpha|,1^{3}](1^{ab-|\\alpha|})\\neq 0$, again by the Murnaghan–Nakayama rule. Thus the characters do not agree, as required.\nL1331: We now assume that $\\lambda$ is not of any one of the above forms (so $\\lambda$ has at least 2 removable nodes and is not a hook or 2-line partition). We set $r_{1}=|{\\rm Rem}(\\lambda)|\\geq 2$, by assumption. We have that $A^{2}(\\lambda)$ contains $[n-2,1^{2}]$ with multiplicity ${r_{1}\\choose 2}\\geq 1$ by cite61†theorem 5.2 .\nL1332: We will show that $\\langle A^{2}([\\lambda])\\mid\\chi_{\\rm hook}\\rangle>{r_{1}\\choose 2}$ and hence deduce that $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ has another, distinct, hook constituent; the result will then follow. Since $\\lambda$ is not itself a hook, we can put together cite93†Propositions 4.2 and cite61†5.2 and hence deduce that\nL1333:  | $\\displaystyle\\langle A^{2}([\\lambda])\\mid\\chi_{\\rm hook}\\rangle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\langle S^{2}([\\lambda])\\mid\\chi_{\\rm hook}\\rangle=1+(r_{1}-1)+{\\textstyle{r_{1}-1}\\choose 2}+\\dots$  |\nL1334: \nL1335: where $\\dots$ denotes the contribution from hook constituents of depth greater than 2. Now, we have that\nL1336: \nL1337:  | $$1+(r_{1}-1)+{\\textstyle{r_{1}-1}\\choose 2}=\\frac{2r_{1}+(r_{1}^{2}-3r_{1}+2)}{2}=\\frac{r_{1}^{2}-r_{1}+2}{2}>\\frac{r_{1}^{2}-r_{1}}{2}={r_{1}\\choose 2}$$  |\nL1338: \nL1339: as required. ∎\nL1340: ###### Remarks 8.2.\nL1341: \nL1342: In [cite36†33 ], Malle and Magaard consider the symmetric and alternating parts of the tensor squares of irreducible modules for the alternating groups in all characteristics, and they classify when these are irreducible.\nL1343: \nL1344: ## 9. Multiplicity-free symmetric and alternating parts\nL1345: \nL1346: Multiplicity-free Kronecker products have been classified in [cite44†5 ]; fortunately, the classification of the multiplicity-free Kronecker squares is much easier, and we recall this here.\nL1347: ###### Proposition 9.1.\nL1348: \nL1349: [cite44†5 , Proposition 4.1] Let $\\lambda$ be a partition of $n$. Then $[\\lambda]^{2}$ is multiplicity-free if and only if $\\lambda$ is one of the following (up to conjugation):\nL1350: \nL1351:  | $$(n),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor)\\>.$$  |\nL1352: Obviously, in the cases arising above, both $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ and $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ are multiplicity-free, and we have seen in all cases how the Kronecker square decomposes into the symmetric and alternating part. In fact we find:\nL1353: ###### Proposition 9.2.\nL1354: \nL1355: Let $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$.\nL1356: \nL1357:   1. (1)\nL1358: \nL1359: If $\\lambda$ (or its conjugate) is one of the partitions\nL1360: \nL1361:  | $$(n),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor)$$  |\nL1362: \nL1363: then $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ is multiplicity-free.\nL1364: \nL1365:   2. (2)\nL1366: \nL1367: If $\\lambda\\in P(n)$ is such that $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ is multiplicity-free then $\\lambda$ (or its conjugate) is a rectangle or one of the partitions\nL1368: \nL1369:  | $$(n-1,1),\\text{or }(k+1,k)\\>\\text{with }n=2k+1.$$  |\nL1370: \nL1371:   3. (3)\nL1372: If $\\lambda$ (or its conjugate) is one of the partitions\nL1373: \nL1374:  | $$\\begin{array}[]{l}\\displaystyle(n),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,1^{2}),\\;(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor),\\\\[5.0pt]\nL1375: \\text{or one of the exceptional partitions }(5,3),(3^{3}),\\end{array}$$  |\nL1376: \nL1377: then $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ is multiplicity-free (or zero).\nL1378: \nL1379:   4. (4)\nL1380: \nL1381: If $\\lambda\\in P(n)$ is such that $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ is multiplicity-free then $\\lambda$ has at most 2 removable nodes.\nL1382: ###### Proof.\nL1383: Parts (1) and (3): By the comments above and the results in this section, we have already seen that for $(n),\\;(n-1,1),(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor)$ (and their conjugates) the Kronecker square is multiplicity-free, and for the partitions $(n-2,2)$ and $(n-2,1^{2})$ (and their conjugates) the alternating part of the square is multiplicity-free.\nL1384: The case $\\lambda=(5,3)$ follows from Theorem cite134†5.5 , and the remaining case $\\lambda=(3^{3})$ was checked by direct computation.\nL1385: Parts (2) and (4) are an immediate consequence of Theorem cite61†5.2 . ∎\nL1386: \nL1387: We are now able to classify the situations in which both the symmetric and alternating parts are multiplicity-free:\nL1388: ###### Theorem 9.3.\nL1389: \nL1390: Let $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$, $\\lambda\\in P(n)$. Then both $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ and $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ are multiplicity-free if and only if $\\lambda$ (or its conjugate) is one of the partitions\nL1391: \nL1392:  | $$(n),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rfloor).$$  |\nL1393: ###### Proof.\nL1394: \nL1395: We already know that for the partitions $\\lambda$ listed in the assertion the symmetric and alternating part of $[\\lambda]^{2}$ are multiplicity-free.\nL1396: \nL1397: Conversely, we now assume that both parts of $[\\lambda]^{2}$ are multiplicity-free, but that $\\lambda$ is not of one of the listed forms. By Proposition cite135†9.2 , we only have to consider rectangular partitions $\\lambda$ of diagonal length at least 3.\nL1398: Since $\\lambda$ is not a hook, the proof of [cite44†5 , Proposition 4.1] immediately shows that $[\\lambda]^{2}$ has a constituent of multiplicity at least 3, unless possibly $(3^{3})\\subseteq\\lambda$. Thus (at least) one of $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ and $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ has a constituent with multiplicity greater than or equal to 2.\nL1399: For $\\lambda=(3^{3})$, the product $[\\lambda]^{2}$ does not contain any constituent with multiplicity equal to 3, however we can directly compute $\\mbox{$\\langle S^{2}([3^{3}]),[5,2^{2}]\\rangle$}=\\mbox{$\\langle([3^{3}])^{2},[5,2^{2}]\\rangle$}=2$.\nL1400: We can now assume that $\\lambda\\supset(3^{3})$ and $\\ell(\\lambda)\\geq 4$. Set $\\widetilde{\\lambda}=\\lambda\\cap(3^{4})$; then $\\widetilde{\\lambda}=(3^{3},j)$, $j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, and in all these cases $[\\widetilde{\\lambda}]^{2}$ has a constituent of multiplicity 3, hence by the monotonicity of Kronecker coefficients, $[\\lambda]^{2}$ has a constituent of multiplicity at least 3. ∎\nL1401: \nL1402: Computational data lead to the following stronger classification conjecture.\nL1403: ###### Conjecture 9.4.\nL1404: \nL1405: The partitions listed in Proposition cite135†9.2 (1) and (3) are all the partitions where the symmetric or the alternating part of the square is multiplicity-free.\nL1406: ###### Remarks 9.5.\nL1407: \nL1408: (1) If the conjecture holds, then a multiplicity-free symmetric part $S^{2}([\\lambda])$ implies that $[\\lambda]^{2}$ is multiplicity-free, while a multiplicity-free alternating part $A^{2}([\\lambda])$ implies that the coefficients in $[\\lambda]^{2}$ are at most 2.\nL1409: \nL1410: (2) From Theorem cite109†4.1 we already know that no further hooks can appear in the classification.\nL1411: ## 10. Splitting the square: Refining the Saxl Conjecture\nL1412: \nL1413: As we want to discuss refinements of the Saxl conjecture, we first formally recall the conjecture:\nL1414: ###### Conjecture 10.1.\nL1415: \nL1416: (Saxl’s conjecture.) For any $k\\in\\mathbb{N}$, the Kronecker square $[\\rho_{k}]^{2}$ contains all $[\\lambda]\\in{\\rm Irr}(S_{n})$ as constituents.\nL1417: As mentioned in the introduction, there is a conjecture (not restricted to triangular numbers) due to Heide, Saxl, Tiep and Zalesski [cite66†22 ], which says that for any $n\\neq 2,4,9$ there is some character $\\psi\\in{\\rm Irr}(S_{n})$ such that $\\psi^{2}$ contains all $\\chi\\in{\\rm Irr}(S_{n})$ as constituents. The Saxl conjecture suggests a special candidate for triangular numbers $n$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.07446v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"
